<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  THE SHIPPED OAUTH CLIENTS.

  This is how a desktop app signs you in with one tap: it carries its own registered OAuth client,
  opens your browser at the provider, and takes the redirect back. Claude Desktop, gcloud, gh, and
  every CLI that "just works" does exactly this — the client ID is compiled in, and the user never
  sees it.

  A client ID for a native/installed app is NOT a secret. Google, OpenAI and the OAuth specs all say
  so plainly: it names the app asking for consent, it is visible in every authorize URL, and it
  cannot be used to reach any API on its own. That is why it can live in a resource file in the open
  and why PKCE (RFC 7636), not client secrecy, is what protects the flow.

  WHEN A VALUE HERE IS NON-EMPTY, THAT PROVIDER'S ROW BECOMES A SINGLE BUTTON — no Client ID box,
  ever, for anyone installing this build.

  WHEN IT IS EMPTY, the row honestly says a client has to be created and offers a one-tap link to
  the exact page that creates it. Empty is the shipped state here because Masamune has no client
  registered with these providers yet; filling one in is a config change, not a code change.

  To fill one in for Google:
    console.cloud.google.com/apis/credentials → Create credentials → OAuth client ID
    → application type "TV and Limited Input devices"  (this is the type the device grant accepts,
      and it requires no redirect URI at all)
    → paste the client ID between the tags below.

  For OpenAI: platform.openai.com → an OAuth app with http://127.0.0.1/callback allowed as a
  loopback redirect. RFC 8252 §7.3 has servers ignore the port on loopback, so no port is listed.
-->
<resources>
    <string name="oauth_client_google" translatable="false"></string>
    <string name="oauth_client_openai" translatable="false"></string>

    <!-- Deep links to the exact page that creates a client, so "create one" is one tap. -->
    <string name="oauth_console_google" translatable="false">https://console.cloud.google.com/apis/credentials</string>
    <string name="oauth_console_openai" translatable="false">https://platform.openai.com/settings/organization/general</string>
</resources>
